  "sections": [
    {
      "key": "quickFacts",
      "source": "scotcat",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.scotcat.com/amphiliidae/amphilius_platychir.htm",
      "html": "<ul><li>Common name: Mountain barbel</li></ul>"
    },
    {
      "key": "quickFacts",
      "source": "planetcatfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.planetcatfish.com/common/species.php?species_id=1417",
      "html": "<ul><li>Scientific name: Amphilius platychir (Günther, 1864)</li><li>Common name: Mountain Barbel</li><li>Type locality: Sierra Leone</li><li>IUCN Red List: Least Concern (last assessed 2019)</li></ul>"
    },
    {
      "key": "beforeYouBuy",
      "source": "scotcat",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.scotcat.com/amphiliidae/amphilius_platychir.htm",
      "html": "<p>This species and probably most of this genus can easily be misidentified, and the thumbnails may show this species or closely related species such as A. atesuensis or A. uranoscopus. They are all more or less the same when kept in aquaria. It is not imported very often but would make a good aquarium addition if kept in optimum conditions.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "distribution",
      "source": "scotcat",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.scotcat.com/amphiliidae/amphilius_platychir.htm",
      "html": "<p>Africa: Sierra Leone, Guinea, Côte d’Ivoire. Type locality: Sierra Leone.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "distribution",
      "source": "planetcatfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.planetcatfish.com/common/species.php?species_id=1417",
      "html": "<p>Africa: Sierra Leone, Guinea and Côte d'Ivoire. Reports from Ghana and Tanzania need confirmation. Known from the Senegal, Niger, Gambia, Corubal, Konkouré and Little Scarcies Rivers. Reported from Luongo River, Congo system, Zambia.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "habitat",
      "source": "scotcat",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.scotcat.com/amphiliidae/amphilius_platychir.htm",
      "html": "<p>The genus inhabits flowing waters in its natural habitat.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "habitat",
      "source": "planetcatfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.planetcatfish.com/common/species.php?species_id=1417",
      "html": "<p>Most amphiliid species are found in fast-flowing water of clear, rocky streams at high elevations or rapids of large lowland rivers. Species of Amphilius are adapted for fast flow and rocky habitats. Morphological adaptations for these habitats include expanded pectoral and pelvic fins with a thickened first ray, depressed body, dorsally directed eyes and reduced swim bladder. The large pelvic fins form, in conjunction with the body, a feeble sucking disc that enables the fish to cling to rocks.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "waterConditions",
      "source": "scotcat",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.scotcat.com/amphiliidae/amphilius_platychir.htm",
      "html": "<p>Water would need to be kept on the cool side for this genus, as they inhabit flowing waters in their natural habitat. This genus prefers cooler temperatures in the aquarium. Temperature: 23–26°C. pH: 6.5–7.5.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "equipment",
      "source": "scotcat",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.scotcat.com/amphiliidae/amphilius_platychir.htm",
      "html": "<p>Can be kept in aquaria with a small-grained gravel or sand substrate, well planted, with a slight current to mimic their natural habitat. Provide rockwork and caves.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "diet",
      "source": "scotcat",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.scotcat.com/amphiliidae/amphilius_platychir.htm",
      "html": "<p>Mosquito larvae, Tubifex, Cyclops, Daphnia, grindal worms, vegetable food, tablet and flake.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "diet",
      "source": "planetcatfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.planetcatfish.com/common/species.php?species_id=1417",
      "html": "<p>The diet of Amphilius species has been reported to consist predominantly of benthic aquatic insects.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "reproduction",
      "source": "planetcatfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.planetcatfish.com/common/species.php?species_id=1417",
      "html": "<p>There is no breeding report.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "notes",
      "source": "scotcat",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.scotcat.com/amphiliidae/amphilius_platychir.htm",
      "html": "<p>Etymology: The specific name platychir: platy, flat or broad; cheiros, hand, referring to the broad, fan-like pectoral-fin rays.</p><p>IUCN Red List: this species is widespread with no known major threats and is classified as Least Concern. With research suggesting undescribed cryptic species within the A. platychir complex, additional research on the taxonomy and distribution of this species is needed (IUCN 2020).</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "references",
      "source": "scotcat",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.scotcat.com/amphiliidae/amphilius_platychir.htm",
      "html": "<p>Diouf, K. &amp; Entsua-Mensah, M. 2020. Amphilius platychir. The IUCN Red List of Threatened Species 2020.</p><p>Ferraris, C.J. Jr., 2007. Checklist of catfishes, recent and fossil (Osteichthyes: Siluriformes), and catalogue of siluriform primary types. Zootaxa 1418:1-628.</p><p>Seegers, Lothar; The Catfishes of Africa. A handbook for identification and maintenance. Tetra Verlag GmbH. 604p.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "references",
      "source": "planetcatfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.planetcatfish.com/common/species.php?species_id=1417",
      "html": "<p>Catalogue of the fishes in the British Museum v. 5, pp 134.</p>"
    }
